.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt ACCEPT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm accept
.Nd accept a queued connection
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/socket.h
.Ft int
.Fn accept "int fd" "struct sockaddr *restrict addr" "socklen_t *restrict addrlen"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn accept
removes a completed connection from the accept queue of
.Fa fd
and returns a new descriptor for it.
.Fa fd
must refer to a listening TCP socket.
The new descriptor is blocking and has close-on-exec clear.
.Pp
If the queue is empty, the call waits for a connection.
On a listener with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
set, an empty queue instead gives
.Er EAGAIN .
An unblocked, nonignored pending signal interrupts the wait with
.Er EINTR .
.Pp
If both
.Fa addr
and
.Fa addrlen
are non-null, the call reports the peer's IPv4 address and port.
Before the call,
.Pf * Fa addrlen
is the capacity of
.Fa addr
in bytes.
At most that many bytes of the 16-byte
.Vt sockaddr_in
are copied, and
.Pf * Fa addrlen
is set to 16 even if the result was truncated.
The family is in host byte order; the address and port are in network
byte order.
If either pointer is null, no address is returned.
An address-copy failure closes the new descriptor and discards the
dequeued connection.
.Pp
The accept queue holds at most 15 completed connections.
The TCP listener drops an incoming SYN when the free-frame count is at
or below the memory reserve, before allocating the connection.
Such a drop produces no error from
.Fn accept .
.Pp
The kernel interface is
.Xr socketcall 2
operation 5.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
On success,
.Fn accept
returns the new descriptor.
On failure it returns -1 and sets
.Va errno .
The raw kernel call returns a negative error number on failure.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default-suite probe
.Pa tests/loopprobe.c
binds a TCP listener to port 7200, connects a second socket to
.Ql 127.0.0.1:7200 ,
accepts with both address pointers null, and exchanges data through the
accepted connection.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EOPNOTSUPP
.It Er EAGAIN
The listener is nonblocking and its accept queue is empty.
.It Er EFAULT
The socketcall argument array cannot be read, or the peer address or
length cannot be read or written as required.
.It Er EINTR
A signal interrupted the wait.
.It Er EINVAL
The socket is a datagram or a stream that is not listening.
.It Er EMFILE
No descriptor or open-file description could be allocated for the
connection.
The dequeued connection is discarded.
.It Er ENOTSOCK
.Fa fd
is invalid or does not refer to a socket.
.It Er EOPNOTSUPP
.Fa fd
refers to a raw socket.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bind 2 ,
.Xr connect 2 ,
.Xr listen 2 ,
.Xr socket 2 ,
.Xr socketcall 2 ,
.Xr memory 7
